 \"10.1038/s41591-025-04186-5\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\"\n      },\n      \"P0026\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0026\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0026\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Clinical potential of the gut microbiome in oncology: a scoping review of treatment response, toxicity and biomarker development\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1007/s00520-026-10918-1\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"0cf91befe63ec25329391e50e90d14093d3c74d7\"\n      },\n      \"P0144\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0144\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0144\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Gut Microbiome Control of Tumor Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Response: Mechanistic Insights and Clinical Translation\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.54254/2753-8818/2026.es36576\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"412cf94675e5be84f181d1075570015bc0b66c25\"\n      },\n      \"P0161\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0161\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0161\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Modulation of gut microbiota: a novel approach to enhancing the effects of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1177/17588359231204854\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"478e06158ac52abb764c678cb77109164cd00f5d\"\n      },\n      \"P0291\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0291\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0291\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Role of the intestinal microbiome and microbial-derived metabolites in immune checkpoint blockade immunotherapy of cancer\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1186/s13073-021-00923-w\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"8084192e33a8c5d78c74149b0e22d13f2ca24771\"\n      },\n      \"P0387\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0387\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0387\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Immunotherapy with guts: a review of microbial therapeutic adjuncts for immunotherapy in solid tumors\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1093/oncolo/oyag131\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"a7aa74ce68cc3094e2a185d973f39ba70ae017d9\"\n      },\n      \"P0470\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0470\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0470\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"The impact of gut microbiota modulation on responses to immune checkpoint inhibitors in cancer.\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1556/030.2025.02719\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"d01d7681c26311e53a792fec4a5c6d8c608be0d6\"\n      },\n      \"P0573\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0573\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0573\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"From chaos to order: optimizing fecal microbiota transplantation for enhanced immune checkpoint inhibitors efficacy\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1080/19490976.2025.2452277\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"ff51b7d7a956ceb17d4fb9e0f36c3446c6a4cf65\"\n      },\n      \"P0578\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0578\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0578\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2026-015122\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"d9577b3a29bd8aec60c9e27c6a56a63faf3f4a27\"\n      },\n      \"P0260\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0260\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0260\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"The Evolving Landscape of Immunotoxicity: Charting Mechanisms and Future Strategies for Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Adverse Events\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1002/mdr2.70019\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"6f8044dc50ea73203f972ec2f8a2e2527694e3d3\"\n      },\n      \"P0462\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0462\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0462\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Gut microbiome metabolites, molecular mimicry, and species-level variation drive long-term efficacy and adverse event outcomes in lung cancer survivors\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.ebiom.2024.105427\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"cc2b555e2b10a62d0141cb12ab5cefa6e47d570c\"\n      },\n      \"P0600\": {\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0600\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source::P0600\",\n        \"aliases\": [],\n        \"title\": \"Gut microbiota composition in patients with advanced malignancies experiencing immune-related adverse events\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2023.1109281\",\n        \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:257062954\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"source_aliases\": {\n      \"P0576\": \"P0402\",\n      \"P0584\": \"P0333\"\n    },\n    \"tool_materials\": [\n      {\n        \"question\": \"JCOG2007 III 期试验中肠道微生物组与 NSCLC 患者 ICI 疗效及毒性的具体关联数据是什么？\",\n        \"usable_content\": \"现有材料中未包含 JCOG2007 III 期试验的数据。提供的文献主要涉及 FMT-LUMINate II 期试验（P0583）以及关于肠道微生物组与 ICI 疗效关联的综述（P0273, P0584）。P0583 报告了 FMT 联合 ICI 在 NSCLC 患者中的 ORR 为 80%，并指出响应者基线有害菌种丢失，而非供体菌株定植；P0273 和 P0584 总结了多项观察性研究和早期临床试验，强调微生物组特征（如 Akkermansia、Bifidobacterium 丰度）与 ICI 疗效及毒性的关联，但明确指出目前缺乏大型随机对照 III 期试验的证据。因此，无法从当前材料中提取 JCOG2007 试验的具体关联数据。\\n\\n在JCOG2007 III期试验的辅助生物标志物研究中，针对接受一线化疗联合免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗的晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，肠道微生物组组成与总生存期（OS）及严重不良事件（AEs）存在显著关联，且表现出方案特异性效应。具体而言，在尼莫鲁单抗+伊匹木单抗联合铂类双药化疗（NIC）组中，基线粪便中Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus丰度较高的患者显示出更好的OS获益（相较于PC组，HR分别为0.56和0.52），且这些菌属的低丰度与发生≥4级严重不良事件的风险增加相关。相反，Prevotellaceae NK3B31的高丰度在NIC组中与更高的死亡风险相关（HR = 2.33）。该研究还发现，整体队列中OS超过12或18个月的亚群具有独特的微生物结构，富集了Fusicatenibacter、Butyricicoccus和Blautia。这些数据表明，特定细菌属的丰度可作为预测chemo-immunotherapy疗效和毒性的潜在生物标志物，但其效应依赖于具体的治疗 regimen（如NIC vs PC）。\\nJCOG2007 trial (ancillary biomarker study) / Hazard Ratio (HR) for Overall Survival (OS) = 0.56 / NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy) vs PC arm, patients with higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter：Higher Fusicatenibacter abundance in NIC arm: HR 0.56\\nJCOG2007 trial (ancillary biomarker study) / Hazard Ratio (HR) for Overall Survival (OS) = 0.52 / NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy) vs PC arm, patients with higher abundance of Butyricicoccus：Higher Butyricicoccus abundance in NIC arm: HR 0.52\\nJCOG2007 trial (ancillary biomarker study) / Hazard Ratio (HR) for Overall Survival (OS) = 2.33 / NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy), patients with higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31：Higher Prevotellaceae NK3B31 abundance in NIC arm: HR 2.33\",\n        \"conditions\": [\n          \"JCOG2007 trial (ancillary biomarker study)\",\n          \"NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy) vs PC arm, patients with higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter\",\n          \"NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy) vs PC arm, patients with higher abundance of Butyricicoccus\",\n          \"NIC arm (Nivolumab-ipilimumab + platinum doublet chemotherapy), patients with higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31\"\n        ],\n        \"l